{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 43, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades and a powerful ruler in the Levant. Born around 1134 he inherited the county upon the death of his father Raymond II. His reign was marked by both military campaigns and diplomatic maneuvering aimed at maintaining Tripoli's independence and prosperity.\n\n*Early Life and Accession to the County*\n\nRaymond III's early life was shaped by the turbulent political landscape of the Levant. His father Raymond II had worked diligently to consolidate Tripoli's position amidst competing powers like the Zengids and the Crusaders. Raymond III received military training and political education preparing him for leadership. Upon his father's death in 1154 Raymond ascended to the county facing immediate challenges to his authority. He had to navigate internal rivalries and external threats from Muslim rulers. He skillfully established alliances and maintained a strong military presence to secure his rule.\n\nRaymond III’s reign saw considerable military activity. He engaged in frequent conflicts with the Zengid dynasty particularly under Nur ad-Din and later Saladin. These conflicts involved sieges raids and strategic alliances with other Crusader states. He also fought against various Muslim emirs and local rulers aiming to expand Tripoli’s influence and protect its trade routes. His military successes helped to maintain Tripoli's autonomy and economic stability. He also dealt with internal rebellions and maintained control over the county's diverse population.\n\n*Diplomacy and Foreign Policy*\n\nRaymond III was a shrewd diplomat adept at forming alliances and negotiating treaties. He sought to balance relationships with both Christian and Muslim powers. He engaged in diplomatic exchanges with the Papacy and other Crusader rulers seeking support and assistance. He also maintained communication with Muslim rulers sometimes engaging in trade agreements or temporary truces. His diplomatic efforts were crucial in navigating the complex political landscape of the Levant and ensuring Tripoli's survival. He understood the importance of maintaining good relations with neighboring states even those with whom he was in conflict.\n\n*Legacy and Death*\n\nRaymond III died in 1199 after a long and eventful reign. He left behind a county that was relatively prosperous and independent despite the constant threats from Muslim forces. He was remembered as a capable military leader a skilled diplomat and a devoted ruler. His reign helped to solidify Tripoli's position as a major trading center and a vital link between Europe and the East. His death marked the end of an era and ushered in a period of increasing instability in the Levant. His policies and actions had a lasting impact on the history of the County of Tripoli and the Crusades.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2371193766593933}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.20648594200611115}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.054002758115530014}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.0002401778765488416}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.8371408916427754e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -7.152531907195225e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.58813522325363e-05}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.002115513663738966}, {"token": "▁until", "logprob": -0.028876617550849915}, {"token": "▁his", "logprob": -0.00027807659353129566}, {"token": "▁death", "logprob": -1.537788011773955e-05}, {"token": "▁in", "logprob": -4.398726014